# What Caddy runs when .env has a CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN (docker-compose.prod.yml
# picks this file then, and Dockerfile.prod builds Caddy with the plugin it
# needs): everything in Caddyfile, plus one wildcard certificate for every
# workspace subdomain.
#
# Without it, each {slug}.APP_BASE_DOMAIN gets its own certificate on first
# visit: the first request waits for it, Let's Encrypt allows about 50 new
# ones a week per registered domain (past that, Caddy falls back to ZeroSSL),
# and every certificate is published in Certificate Transparency logs, which
# then list every workspace's name. A wildcard can only be proven over DNS, so
# Caddy writes the challenge's TXT record through the Cloudflare API with the
# token, once now and again at each renewal.
#
# Caddy picks the most specific site, so {slug}.APP_BASE_DOMAIN lands here and
# never reaches the on-demand ask. The base domain itself, nested subdomains
# and custom domains still match the catch-all site in Caddyfile, gated by
# AuthorizeTlsHost as before.
import Caddyfile

*.{$APP_BASE_DOMAIN} {
	import app

	tls {
		dns cloudflare {env.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}
		# Docker's own resolver can answer from a cache made before the TXT
		# record existed, and Caddy waits until it sees the record; Cloudflare's
		# public resolvers see its own zones at once (the plugin's
		# troubleshooting notes recommend this).
		resolvers 1.1.1.1 1.0.0.1
	}
}
